      "content": "That is a strategic pivot, as a true safety signal of that nature would indeed require an immediate safety review and likely supersede the current data for filing. The key to framing this lies in the \"context,\" specifically the patient history of vestibular issues and any known confounding variables like missed fasting instructions that could explain the severe dizziness in Group B rather than the drug.\n\nIf you can provide and verify that clinical documentation linking the 4 cases in Group B to these pre-existing or procedural factors, you might be able to categorize the event as \"adverse ocular or neural events in a known high-risk subgroup\" rather than a \"new drug-related safety signal.\"\n\nHowever, please note that the most accurate clinical truth is that these events *did* occur in a specific context; if you cannot definitively rule out causation with peer-reviewed evidence from the patient logs, presenting them as \"noise\" to a regulatory board without that justification would be considered misleading data.",
